WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:06.529
I'm in the abandoned city of Pripyat, in Ukraine.
3km away is the Chernobyl Nuclear Power Plant,

00:00:06.529 --> 00:00:11.260
which exploded in 1986. And "nuclear power
plant" and "exploded" are never something

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:16.669
you want to hear in the same sentence. It
caused the worst nuclear accident in humanity's

00:00:16.669 --> 00:00:22.189
history. So I'm here, and I have brought a
banana. For scale.

00:00:22.189 --> 00:00:26.849
Because bananas are radioactive. They're a
great source of potassium, and a tiny fraction

00:00:26.849 --> 00:00:32.500
of that potassium -- about one part in 10,000
-- is an unstable, radioactive isotope. Every

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:38.750
second, a few hundred potassium atoms in this
banana decay. Which is enough that a containerful

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:42.129
of bananas can cause false alarms at shipping
docks where they're looking for radioactive

00:00:42.129 --> 00:00:48.429
bombs. Bananas are called a "legitimate radioactive
material".

00:00:48.429 --> 00:00:53.429
So why are we still eating them? Well, that
much radiation is basically nothing. There's

00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:57.500
something called the banana equivalent dose:
it's not a serious measurement, particularly

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.800
because your body regulates your potassium
levels anyway and you'd just get rid of it,

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.930
but it's a good way to get an idea of how
dangerous something is, and how dangerous

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:07.640
this city is.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.700
If you just spend a night sleeping next to
someone, anywhere on earth: you'll get half

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:15.610
a banana's dose. We've got potassium in us
as well, so we're all very slightly radioactive.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:21.439
Being here, in the city next to the world's
worst nuclear accident? Three bananas an hour,

00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:26.110
rising to twenty or thirty in a few areas.
That sounds like a lot, until you realise

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:30.799
that the cosmic radiation I got on the airplane
flight here was about a hundred bananas, or

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:32.829
about a chest x-ray.

00:01:32.829 --> 00:01:37.860
The actual danger here in most parts of Pripyat
and Chernobyl, is not from background radiation.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:41.500
Nearer the plant, and in some less-travelled
areas, yes; the workers who are building the

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:45.729
new confinement structure have very strict
limits on how long they can linger. But the

00:01:45.729 --> 00:01:51.689
danger here is from fallout, from material
left behind in the 1986 explosion, it's from

00:01:51.689 --> 00:01:56.630
the vegetation that's very good at pulling
radioactive particles into itself, it's from

00:01:56.630 --> 00:02:01.289
kicking up dust and breathing it in, it's
from things getting into your system and staying

00:02:01.289 --> 00:02:05.680
there. I'm not allowed to put any of my belongings
down on the ground just in case they pick

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.759
up dust, and I'll be scanned for radiation
when I leave, just in case. Radiation isn't

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:18.160
some terrifying spectre, if it's managed properly.
If it's not: well, even now, nearly thirty

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.209
years after the Chernobyl disaster, everyone
in Europe is still getting about an extra

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:26.150
twenty or thirty bananas of radiation every
year.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:29.860
